      "published_response_sha256": "e34366427a3353200914cf684300688fd1235eea708c4d5b4140e172ead9d0e4",
      "answer_text_sha256": "d5a970813f129c5fa4154ace8f5025c52f2b9c950b1d7e31ce67a8b64150fdc6",
      "answer_text": "The light-dependent reactions use certain molecules, called energy carriers, to temporarily store energy, and these carriers move energy from the light-dependent reactions to the light-independent reactions [ev_005]. Photosystems absorb light and use electron transport chains to convert energy into the chemical energy of ATP and NADH, as this passage states [ev_002]. The overall purpose of the light-dependent reactions is to convert light energy into chemical energy, and this chemical energy will be used by the Calvin cycle to fuel the assembly of sugar molecules [ev_008]. The energy-carrying molecules ATP and NADPH travel into the stroma, where the Calvin cycle reactions take place [ev_007]. The carriers that move energy from the light-dependent reactions to the Calvin cycle are described as \"full\" because they bring energy; after the energy is released, the \"empty\" carriers return to the light-dependent reactions to obtain more energy [ev_001]. In the Calvin cycle, the energy in ATP and NADPH is used to convert three-carbon molecules into G3P [ev_010].",
